ficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d39c521-092e-5b02-9c9d-873965d15a2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-342644" :question/problem "$$\nx(x-9)(x+1){y}^{″}+(x-9){y}^{′}+x(x+1)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y=0\n$$\n\nClassify the following points for the differential equation given above.$\\,$The point $x=3$ is\n{{field-1}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\nFor $x=-1$, select its classification or real-domain status:\n{{field-2}}\n\n$\\,$The point $x=0$ is\n{{field-3}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=9$ is\n{{field-4}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\nWork over the reals. Use the classification \"outside the real coefficient domain\" at points separated from the coefficient domain. At a boundary of the coefficient domain, classify singularity by whether the normalized coefficients, or their required weighted forms, admit analytic extension from that domain." :question/worked-solution "For the original real logarithm $\\ln x$, the coefficient domain is $x>0$. Normalize the equation on that domain away from its zero leading coefficient:\n\n$$y''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0,\\qquad P(x)=\\frac1{x(x+1)},\\quad Q(x)=\\frac{\\ln x}{x-9}.$$\n\nAt $x=3$, both normalized coefficients are analytic, so the point is ordinary.\n\nThe point $x=-1$ lies outside the real coefficient domain. There is no real neighborhood of $-1$ on which $\\ln x$ is defined, and it is not a boundary point approached from $x>0$. Therefore the correct classification is \"outside the real coefficient domain\". Calling it a real regular or irregular singular point would presuppose a local differential equation that is not defined there.\n\nThe point $x=0$ is a singular boundary point. From $x>0$, $xP=1/(x+1)$ has an analytic extension, but $x^2Q=x^2\\ln x/(x-9)$ does not: its second derivative has leading divergent term $-(2/9)\\ln x$. Therefore zero is irregular singular under the stated analytic extension criterion. A finite weighted-coefficient limit alone does not establish analyticity.\n\nAt $x=9$, $Q$ has a pole, while $(x-9)P$ and $(x-9)^2Q=(x-9)\\ln x$ are analytic. Therefore $x=9$ is regular singular.\n\nReplacing the original coefficient by $\\ln|x|$ would define a different equation and make $-1$ regular singular."}]}
